<p style="text-align: justify;"><div class="img-block"><img src="./Course Syllabus Microsoft .NET Framework and C# Programming Language Version 5.0.0 __ Course Syllabus Microsoft .NET Framework and C# Programming Language_files/Шаблон РПО C# En.jpg" class="fr-fic fr-dib back-process" id="img2"></div></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>For Part-time groups</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Version 5.0.0</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Course duration — 13 lessons</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Course objective</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">To teach students the basics of application development using the Microsoft .NET framework and the C# programming language<strong>.&nbsp;</strong>To<strong>&nbsp;</strong>teach<strong>&nbsp;them to apply</strong> the right mechanisms and constructs to solve a particular problem.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><strong>Upon completion of the course, students will:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Understand the causes of occurence of the Microsoft .NET platform;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Use the basic terms of the Microsoft .NET platform: CLR, CLS, CTS, BCL;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Use reflectors and dotfuscators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Understand the intricacies of OOP implementation in C#;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Create custom exception classes;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Create custom delegates and events;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Interact with the garbage collector;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Interact with inherited code;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Use serialization mechanisms;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Apply logging tools;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Know the basics of using design patterns and UML.</li>
</ul>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;">Upon completion of this course, studentssubmit a practical assignment and a theory exam on the course materials. To be admitted to the exam, all homework and practical assignments must be submitted. The practical assignment should cover the maximum of material from different sections of the course.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<h2 style="text-align: justify;" id="h-H2_0"><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Topic Plan</span></h2>
<table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" class="fr-alternate-rows fr-remove-borders">
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 1.</strong> Introduction to Microsoft .NET. Data Types. Operators</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 2.</strong> Arrays and Strings. Enumerations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 3.</strong> Structures. Classes. Properties</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 4.</strong> Inheritance and Polymorphism</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 5.</strong> Operator Overloading</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lesson <strong>6.</strong> Interfaces</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 7.</strong> Exception Handling. Garbage Collection</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 8.</strong> Generics, Using Generic Collections</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 9.</strong> Delegates, Events. LINQ</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 10.</strong> Interaction with a File System. Serialization</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 11.</strong> Using Inherited Program Code</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson 12.</strong> Introduction to UML and Design Patterns</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lesson&nbsp;</strong><strong>13</strong>. Exam</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><br></p>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><em>&nbsp;</em></p>
<h2 style="text-align: left;" id="h-H2_1">Lesson 1.<br>
<strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Introduction to Microsoft .NET.&nbsp;</span></strong><strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Data&nbsp;</span></strong><strong style="font-weight: 700;"><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Types</span></strong><strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">. Operators</span></strong></h2>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: bold;">Introduction to <strong>Microsoft .NET:&nbsp;</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">History and stages of development of programming technologies;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Causes of occurence of the Microsoft .NET platform;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Comparative analysis of advantages and disadvantages of Microsoft .NET.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Basic concepts of Microsoft .NET:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Architecture of Microsoft .NET;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">CLR (Common Language Runtime);</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">CTS (Common Type System);</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">CLS (Common Language Specification);</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Class Library FCL (BCL);</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Languages of Microsoft .NET;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">CIL (Common Intermediate Language);</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Compilation and execution scheme of a Microsoft .NET app;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The concepts of metadata, manifest, assembly.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>.NET Core, .NET Standard:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is .NET Core?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Tasks and objectives of .NET Core;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is .NET Standard?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Tasks and objectives of .NET Standard;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Comparison of .NET Core, .NET Standard, .NET Framework.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Introduction to the C# programming language:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Pros and cons of the C# programming language;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The simplest program in the C# programming language.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Reflectors and dotfuscators:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is a reflector?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The need to use a reflector;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Overview of existing reflectors;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is a dotfuscator?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The need to use dotfuscators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Overview of existing dotfuscators.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Data types:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Integer data types;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Data types for floating-point numbers;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Character data type;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Logical data type.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Nullable types:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is a nullable type;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Tasks and objectives of nullable types;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Operations available for nullable types;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Examples of use.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Literals.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Variables</strong>:
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The concept of a variable;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Rules for naming variables;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Scope of variables.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Input, output in the console application.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Structural and reference types.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;">Conversion of <strong>types:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Explicit conversion;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Implicit conversion.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Operators:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Arithmetic operators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Relational operators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Logical operators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Bitwise operators;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Assignment operator;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Operators precedence;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Null-conditional operator;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Null-coalescing operator.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Conditions:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The if conditional statement;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The if else conditional statement;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The switch conditional statement:
<ul>
<li style="text-align: justify;">switch statement;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">switch expression.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Ternary operator ?:.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Loops:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The for loop;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The while loop;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The do while loop;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The foreach loop;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The break statement;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The continue statement;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The goto statement<em>.&nbsp;</em></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align: justify;" id="h-H2_2">Lesson 2.<br>
<strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Arrays and Strings. Enumeration</span></strong></h2>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Arrays:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">One-dimensional arrays;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Multidimensional arrays;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Ragged arrays;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Using the foreach loop.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Null-conditional and Null-coalescing operators.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Strings:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Creating a string;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Operations with strings;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Features of using strings.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Using command line arguments.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Enumeration (enum):</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The concept of enumeration;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Syntax for declaring an enumeration;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The need and features of using enumeration;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Installing the basic type of enumeration;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Using methods for enumerations.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Top level statements:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What are top level statements?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Rules for using top level statements;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Working with the command line;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Application examples<em>.</em></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align: justify;" id="h-H2_3">Lesson 3.<br>
<strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Structures. Classes. Properties</span></strong></h2>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Structures:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The concept of structure;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Syntax for declaring a structure;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The need and features of using structures;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Constructor without parameters and structure.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Syntax of class declaration.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Access modifiers of the C# programming language.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Fields of the class.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Constructors:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">The concept of constructor;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Parameterized constructor;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Overloaded constructors;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Static constructor.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Keyword this.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Methods of a class:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Passing parameters;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The return keyword;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Method overloading;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Short syntax of single-line methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Using ref and out parameters:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Using the ref modifier;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Using the out modifier;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Carriages.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Creating methods with a varying number of arguments.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Deconstructors.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Partial types.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Properties</strong>:
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What are properties?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Syntax for declaring properties;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">get, set, init;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Short syntax of single-line properties;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Examples of using properties;</li>
<li>Auto-property:
<ul>
<li style="text-align: justify;">What are auto-properties?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Examples of using auto-properties;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Initialization of auto-properties.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Namespace:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is a namespace?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Tasks and objectives of a namespace;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The <span style="color: rgb(63, 63, 64); font-family: Inter, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-style: normal; font-variant-ligatures: normal; font-variant-caps: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; widows: 2; word-spacing: 0px; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-decoration-thickness: initial; text-decoration-style: initial; text-decoration-color: initial; display: inline !important; float: none;">using</span> keyword;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Declaring a namespace;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Nested namespaces;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Decomposition of a namespace;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The default namespace;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The using alias directive;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Applying using to connect static members.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h2 style="text-align: justify;" id="h-H2_4">Lesson 4.<br>
<strong><span style="color: rgb(47, 170, 79);">Inheritance and Polymorphism</span></strong></h2>
<ol>
<li style="font-weight: bold;">Inheritance in<strong>C#:</strong>
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">Analysis of the inheritance mechanism in C#;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Access specifiers in inheritance;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Features of using constructors in inheritance;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The base keyword;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Hiding names in inheritance.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Using the sealed keyword.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Using references to the base class.</strong></li>
<li style="font-weight: bold;"><strong>Polymorphism in C#. Virtual methods</strong>:
<ul style="font-weight: initial;">
<li style="text-align: justify;">What is a virtual method?</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">Overriding virtual methods;</li>
<li style="text-align: justify;">The need to use virtual methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Abstract class.</strong></li>
<li style="text-align: justify; font-weight: bold;"><strong>Analysis of the <strong style="font-weight: 700; color: rgb(63, 63, 64); font-family: Inter, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; font-style: normal; font-variant-ligatures: normal; font-variant-caps: normal; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; widows: 2; word-spacing: 0px; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); text-decoration-thickness: initial; text-decoration-style: initial; text-decoration-color: initial;">Object&nbsp;</strong>base class.</strong></li>
</ol>
